---
title: Network Diagnostics
order: 5
description: ping, traceroute, mtr, ss, netstat, nmap, tcpdump
tags: [ping, traceroute, mtr, ss, netstat, nmap, tcpdump, diagnostics]
---

Tools for testing connectivity, tracing routes, inspecting sockets, scanning
ports, and capturing packets.

@code bash
# ---- ping: test connectivity ----

ping example.com                          # continuous ping (Ctrl-C to stop)
ping -c 4 example.com                    # send 4 packets
ping -c 4 -i 0.2 example.com            # 200ms interval
ping -w 5 example.com                    # timeout after 5 seconds
ping -s 1472 -M do example.com          # test MTU (1472 + 28 header = 1500)
ping -f example.com                      # flood ping (root, careful)
ping -I eth0 example.com                 # bind to interface
ping -t 10 example.com                   # set TTL

# IPv6
ping -6 example.com
ping6 example.com                         # legacy command

@code bash
# ---- traceroute: trace packet path ----

traceroute example.com                    # UDP probes (default)
traceroute -I example.com                # ICMP probes
traceroute -T example.com                # TCP SYN probes (bypasses some filters)
traceroute -p 443 -T example.com         # TCP to specific port
traceroute -n example.com                # no DNS resolution (faster)
traceroute -m 20 example.com             # max 20 hops
traceroute -w 2 example.com              # 2 second timeout per hop

# tracepath: similar, no root needed, finds MTU
tracepath example.com

@code bash
# ---- mtr: combined ping + traceroute ----

mtr example.com                           # interactive (curses) mode
mtr -n example.com                        # no DNS resolution
mtr -r -c 100 example.com               # report mode, 100 cycles
mtr -r -c 100 -w example.com            # wide report (full hostnames)
mtr --tcp -P 443 example.com            # TCP mode, port 443
mtr --udp example.com                    # UDP mode
mtr -e example.com                       # show AS numbers
mtr -z example.com                       # show AS numbers (alternate)
mtr --json example.com                   # JSON output
mtr -s 1472 example.com                 # packet size (MTU test)

@code bash
# ---- ss: socket statistics (replaces netstat) ----

ss -tlnp                                  # TCP listening, numeric, with process
ss -ulnp                                  # UDP listening, numeric, with process
ss -tunap                                 # all TCP+UDP with process info
ss -s                                     # socket summary statistics
ss -t state established                   # established TCP connections
ss -t state time-wait                     # TIME_WAIT connections

# filtering
ss -tn dst :443                           # connections to port 443
ss -tn src :80                            # connections from local port 80
ss -tn 'dport == 22'                      # destination port 22
ss -tn 'sport > 1024'                     # source port > 1024
ss -tn dst 192.168.1.0/24                # connections to subnet

# show more detail
ss -eti                                   # extended TCP info (retransmits, RTT)
ss -m                                     # memory usage per socket

@code bash
# ---- netstat (legacy, from net-tools) ----

netstat -tlnp                             # TCP listening with process
netstat -ulnp                             # UDP listening with process
netstat -anp                              # all connections with process
netstat -rn                               # routing table (use ip route instead)
netstat -s                                # protocol statistics
netstat -i                                # interface statistics

@code bash
# ---- nmap: network scanner ----

# host discovery
nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24                  # ping scan (no port scan)
nmap -sL 192.168.1.0/24                  # list targets (DNS only)

# port scanning
nmap 192.168.1.1                          # top 1000 ports
nmap -p 22,80,443 192.168.1.1           # specific ports
nmap -p 1-1024 192.168.1.1              # port range
nmap -p- 192.168.1.1                     # all 65535 ports
nmap -F 192.168.1.1                      # fast scan (top 100)

# scan types
nmap -sS 192.168.1.1                     # SYN scan (stealthy, default w/ root)
nmap -sT 192.168.1.1                     # TCP connect scan
nmap -sU 192.168.1.1                     # UDP scan
nmap -sV 192.168.1.1                     # version detection
nmap -O 192.168.1.1                      # OS detection
nmap -A 192.168.1.1                      # aggressive (OS + version + scripts)

# output
nmap -oN scan.txt 192.168.1.1           # normal output
nmap -oX scan.xml 192.168.1.1           # XML output
nmap -oG scan.grep 192.168.1.1          # grepable output

# useful combinations
nmap -sS -sV -p- --open 192.168.1.1    # all open ports with versions
nmap -sn -PR 192.168.1.0/24             # ARP discovery (local net)

@code bash
# ---- tcpdump: packet capture ----

# basic capture
tcpdump -i eth0                           # all traffic on interface
tcpdump -i any                            # all interfaces
tcpdump -i eth0 -c 100                   # capture 100 packets
tcpdump -i eth0 -w capture.pcap          # write to file
tcpdump -r capture.pcap                  # read from file

# display options
tcpdump -i eth0 -n                       # no DNS resolution
tcpdump -i eth0 -nn                      # no DNS or port name resolution
tcpdump -i eth0 -v                       # verbose
tcpdump -i eth0 -X                       # hex + ASCII payload
tcpdump -i eth0 -A                       # ASCII payload only
tcpdump -i eth0 -e                       # show MAC addresses

# filters (BPF syntax)
tcpdump -i eth0 host 192.168.1.1                    # specific host
tcpdump -i eth0 src 192.168.1.1                     # source only
tcpdump -i eth0 dst 192.168.1.1                     # destination only
tcpdump -i eth0 port 80                              # specific port
tcpdump -i eth0 tcp                                  # TCP only
tcpdump -i eth0 udp                                  # UDP only
tcpdump -i eth0 icmp                                 # ICMP only
tcpdump -i eth0 'tcp port 80 and host 192.168.1.1'  # combined
tcpdump -i eth0 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0'      # SYN packets
tcpdump -i eth0 not port 22                          # exclude SSH

@text
Tips:
- Use `ss` over `netstat`; it is faster and more featureful.
- mtr is excellent for diagnosing intermittent packet loss on a path.
- Always use `-n` with tcpdump to avoid slow DNS lookups during capture.
- tcpdump captures can be opened in Wireshark for detailed analysis.
- nmap scans against networks you do not own may be illegal or against policy.
